WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:11.600 --> 00:00:18.000
I want you to think of how you approach the idea of the quality of your health

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:21.040
care in this country, in the United States.

00:00:21.040 --> 00:00:24.610
For those of you who are watching this from the United States.

00:00:24.610 --> 00:00:29.300
Typically, you would ask around to make sure that you had a good doctor.

00:00:29.300 --> 00:00:32.870
You would try to get a referral from your existing primary care provider,

00:00:32.870 --> 00:00:34.790
so-called PCP.

00:00:34.790 --> 00:00:37.790
Or would ask your friends who had been in similar situations,

00:00:37.790 --> 00:00:41.700
and triangulate and figure out who the good doctors were.

00:00:41.700 --> 00:00:45.810
But you would also have the support of a variety of institutional mechanisms

00:00:45.810 --> 00:00:49.210
that would ensure that the quality of the doctor that you were seeing

00:00:49.210 --> 00:00:51.200
was sufficiently high, so as to reassure you

00:00:51.200 --> 00:00:54.100
that your care was being put in a good person's hands.

00:00:54.100 --> 00:00:58.070
Not just a competent professional, but also a good ethical person.

00:00:58.070 --> 00:01:02.240
There are lists published monthly, annually,

00:01:02.240 --> 00:01:04.690
semi-annually in the United States on the best doctors,

00:01:04.690 --> 00:01:07.010
and this and that specialty.

00:01:07.010 --> 00:01:10.250
There are, of course, institutions that are ranked and rated

00:01:10.250 --> 00:01:13.640
all the time by different commissions of the US government.

00:01:13.640 --> 00:01:17.610
There are magazines that specialize in ranking doctors and medical care

00:01:17.610 --> 00:01:20.070
facilities on a variety of dimensions.

00:01:20.070 --> 00:01:24.467
There is a veritable cornucopia of different ratings.

00:01:24.467 --> 00:01:26.550
And you might have the opposite problem that you'd

00:01:26.550 --> 00:01:28.633
have in other parts of the world-- that here there

00:01:28.633 --> 00:01:30.670
is too much data to base your decision on.

00:01:30.670 --> 00:01:35.090
But you would not be short of being able to get the right information that you

00:01:35.090 --> 00:01:36.830
need to make a good choice of a doctor.

00:01:36.830 --> 00:01:42.410
That information is woefully missing in most parts of the world.

00:01:42.410 --> 00:01:46.540
You would, of course, still essentially ask your friends and family,

00:01:46.540 --> 00:01:48.450
to figure out who the good doctors were.

00:01:48.450 --> 00:01:51.250
But beyond that, you would have much less assurance

00:01:51.250 --> 00:01:55.340
that the doctor that you are seeing is of acceptably high quality.

00:01:55.340 --> 00:01:57.590
Now, of course, if you were in the privileged classes,

00:01:57.590 --> 00:01:58.964
you would find a way around this.

00:01:58.964 --> 00:02:03.390
But for the vast majority of people in South Asia, in developing countries,

00:02:03.390 --> 00:02:07.420
it is very hard to access a doctor, let alone worry about the possibility

00:02:07.420 --> 00:02:09.970
that the doctor is not credentialed, is not ethical, is not

00:02:09.970 --> 00:02:13.240
sufficiently trained, has not had continued education.

00:02:13.240 --> 00:02:16.620
These are all the niceties that don't even cross the minds of anybody.

00:02:16.620 --> 00:02:19.940
If there is a doctor with a Red Cross sign outside,

00:02:19.940 --> 00:02:22.890
that seems to work just fine.

00:02:22.890 --> 00:02:28.330
This absence of information is what I refer to as an institutional void.

00:02:28.330 --> 00:02:29.280
That's a mouthful.

00:02:29.280 --> 00:02:32.236
But void simply refers to the idea that something is missing.

00:02:32.236 --> 00:02:34.110
Here, the piece of information that's missing

00:02:34.110 --> 00:02:36.610
is reliable information about the doctor.

00:02:36.610 --> 00:02:39.990
How do I find a good primary care provider?

00:02:39.990 --> 00:02:41.820
How do I find a good gastroenterologist?

00:02:41.820 --> 00:02:43.361
How do I find a good ophthalmologist?

00:02:43.361 --> 00:02:44.970
How do I find a good oncologist?

00:02:44.970 --> 00:02:48.690
Depending on what my need is, it is fiendishly difficult.

00:02:48.690 --> 00:02:52.820
In a sense, when you peel back the complexities,

00:02:52.820 --> 00:02:56.330
and the ethical dimensions, and the human dimensions of health care,

00:02:56.330 --> 00:03:00.530
there is a basic elemental need here, which

00:03:00.530 --> 00:03:04.670
is to bring together people who have something to exchange.

00:03:04.670 --> 00:03:07.300
Economists would call this buyers and sellers coming together.

00:03:07.300 --> 00:03:09.340
Here, the buyer is a patient.

00:03:09.340 --> 00:03:12.800
He-- I'm going to use he, since I'm thinking of myself--

00:03:12.800 --> 00:03:15.000
is a buyer of medical services.

00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:17.325
And the doctor is the seller of medical services.

00:03:17.325 --> 00:03:21.940
Now I realize calling them buyers and sellers isn't the most natural way,

00:03:21.940 --> 00:03:24.090
but bear with me for a second.

00:03:24.090 --> 00:03:26.990
To get the buyer and seller to come together,

00:03:26.990 --> 00:03:30.000
we basically need a little bit of information that's credible.

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:33.470
So that the buyer can find the seller, and vice versa.

00:03:33.470 --> 00:03:37.769
That information is actually so taken for granted in different parts

00:03:37.769 --> 00:03:38.310
of the world.

00:03:38.310 --> 00:03:43.210
And so completely difficult to engineer in an institutionalized capacity.

00:03:43.210 --> 00:03:45.300
When you stop to think what it takes to make sure

00:03:45.300 --> 00:03:51.230
that information is reliable, easily accessible, credible, reproducible,

00:03:51.230 --> 00:03:54.600
regeneratable-- to coin a word, for a second.

00:03:54.600 --> 00:03:57.809
You realize that there's a lot of complexity to it.

00:03:57.809 --> 00:04:01.100
And when that information is not there, then you have the most basic breakdown.

00:04:01.100 --> 00:04:04.474
A doctor can't find his patient, and a patient can't find his doctor.

00:04:04.474 --> 00:04:06.140
And medical care does not get delivered.

00:04:06.140 --> 00:04:07.980
And that's a problem.

